  <participating-org role="4" type="24">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Social and Economic Empowerment</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The idea to establish Social and Economic Empowerment of the Disadvantaged Trust was an imitative of the founder Hon Margaret Mkanga who has vast experience of social and people with disabilities issue through working in forums in Tanzania and also beinga person with disability herself since she was born. As a secondary school teacher for several years and an activist for PWDs, she has an opportunity to manage different positions representing people with disabilities. She was b also a representative of people with disabilities to the parliament  of the Tanzania for 15  years. Through all these forums she was able to gain experience  and learn socio-economic and rights challenges and problems facing societies  and people with disabilities in Tanzania andoutside the county.

SEED Trust envision is to identify and assist the underprivileged members of the society, namely the people with disabilities, orphans, widows, children and elderly and undertake development programs.

SEED Trust is aware that PWDsare socially and culturally ostracized in terms of obtaining an appropriate education, entering the workforce, and being accepted in the society. They are also denied theirrights including equal access and control of productive resources and democratic and civic engagement and they experience gender based violence.

SEED Trust believes that disability is not inability. Therefore if there is supportive conducive environment by community structures and favorable legal frameworks, PWDs can be able to support themselves. Since disability is not inability, PWDs should be empowered and given an opportunity to social &#x2013;economic development for themselves as well as national development.

Since its establishment, SEED Trust has implemented various interventions that focused on empowering PWDs to address their social economical challenges, discrimination, marginalization and gender inequality and participation in democratic and civic rights within project geographical areas. Among all interventions, SEED Trustis proud of the following accomplishments:

The organization enhanced entrepreneurial skills among women and other disadvantaged groups in Morogoro and Dodoma regions. The acquired skills enabled them to establish IGAs that improved their livelihoods and consequently reduce dependency over men and increase power to address discrimination, marginalization inequality in their communities.

SEED Trust implemented a project that empowered youth and families of PWDs to lessen the burden of care throughentrepreneurships capacity building skills and provision of revolving funds for IGAs. As a result the beneficiaries were able to establish and run IGAs and small businesses that helped them to improve livelihoods and support their children to school and otherfamily needs.

SEED Trust enhanced the rights of people with disabilities and protection through building the capacity of families, children, and community and school teachers in Morogoro Municipality. In addition to that SEED TRUST provided necessaryfacilities equipment to people with disabilities as per needs and Sensitize and advocate community members to support peoplewith disabilities</narrative>
  </participating-org>
